<p>← LOOKING BACK &nbsp;|&nbsp; LOOKING AHEAD →</p>
<p>Across this unit, we have seen utilitarianism mount a serious case for grounding morality in human happiness. Whether you find it convincing depends on whether you find Mill's responses to the standing objections adequate. Unit 5 brings Nietzsche, who will reject not just utilitarianism but the entire project of grounding ethics in any single principle. Where Mill thinks moral progress is possible through clearer thinking about utility, Nietzsche will argue that morality as we know it is a historical accident built on resentment, and that the most important question is not how to refine it but whether to keep it at all.</p>
